///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
version="version surface_type_generics.lib 0.1.2 Aug_2026";
category="Algebraic Geometry";
info="
LIBRARY:  surface_type_generics.lib
AUTHOR:   GPT-5.5 Pro, generated for stand-alone generic surface constructions.

PURPOSE:
  Build generic representatives of the surface types appearing in a fixed
  catalogue of smooth algebraic surfaces: rational, ruled over an elliptic
  curve, K3 type, Enriques type, abelian type, bielliptic / hyperelliptic
  type, and properly elliptic type.

  This file deliberately does not deform hard-coded ideals.  Each constructor
  starts from a geometric parameter space: determinantal scrolls, projected
  Veronese maps, complete intersections, Segre products of curves, finite
  quotient data, liaison chains, or Rao-module/syzygy data.  The returned
  surface is therefore a generic member of the chosen construction family,
  not a coordinate change of a stored example.

WHAT \"GENERIC\" MEANS HERE:
  The procedures choose random coefficients in an explicitly described family.
  Smoothness and the asserted surface type hold on a non-empty Zariski open
  subset of the parameter space.  A random specialization over a sufficiently
  large field is expected to land in this open subset.  For serious use, run a
  smoothness check afterwards, or use the classification library on the output.

  For old degree/sectional-genus labels, surface_generic_catalogueType records the
  birational/Kodaira type.  Call one of the direct constructors below for a
  generic representative of that type.  Exact embedded parameter spaces for
  most high-degree P^4 models are much more specialized and are usually built
  by liaison or syzygy constructions.  Two related degree-11 Enriques
  constructors are implemented here.  surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_DES_dim34 uses
  Popescu's liaison completion of the older 34-dimensional
  Decker--Ein--Schreyer family.
  surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_MB_dim31 retains the normalized Moore/Bring syzygy
  specialization, whose construction image has dimension at most 31.
  For the actual stored examples use the separate nonGeneralTypeSurfacesP4.lib,
  with nonGeneralTypeSurfaceP4(name); the generic constructors here are distinct.

CHARACTERISTIC WARNINGS:
  * Enriques quotients by an involution are implemented for characteristic not 2.
  * The 34-dimensional degree-11 Enriques liaison constructor is implemented
    over prime fields of characteristic greater than 3.  The separate
    Moore/Bring specialization requires a finite field of characteristic
    different from 2 and 5.  Both need a field large enough for the randomized
    open conditions to be met.
  * The Hesse order-3 bielliptic construction is for characteristic not 3 and,
    for an explicit quotient by invariant sections, over a field containing a
    primitive cube root of unity.  The default routine returns the cover and the
    group action as quotient data.
  * Product, scroll and complete-intersection constructors are characteristic
    independent apart from the usual smoothness discriminants.

REFERENCES, NOT DEPENDENCIES:
  - W. Decker, L. Ein, F.-O. Schreyer, Construction of surfaces in P^4,
    J. Algebraic Geometry 2 (1993), 185-237.
  - S. Popescu, On smooth surfaces of degree >= 11 in P^4, PhD thesis,
    Saarbruecken, 1993, Sections 3.7--3.8.
  - F.-O. Schreyer, Small fields in constructive algebraic geometry,
    Moduli of Vector Bundles, LNPAM 179 (1996), 221-228.
  - H. Abo, K. Ranestad, F.-O. Schreyer,
    NongeneralTypeSurfacesInP4.m2 (2026), especially the procedures
    specialEnriquesSchreyerSurface and schreyerSurfaceFromModule.
  - S. Popescu, Examples of smooth non-general type surfaces in P^4,
    Proc. London Math. Soc. 76 (1998), 257-275.
  - W. Decker, F.-O. Schreyer, Non-general type surfaces in P^4: some remarks
    on bounds and constructions, J. Symbolic Computation 29 (2000), 545-582.
  - W. Barth, K. Hulek, C. Peters, A. Van de Ven, Compact Complex Surfaces,
    2nd enlarged ed., Springer, 2004.
  - F. Cossec, I. Dolgachev, Enriques Surfaces I, Birkhaeuser, 1989.
  - R. Miranda, Persson-type constructions and the standard quotient picture
    for bielliptic surfaces; equivalently, every bielliptic surface is a finite
    free quotient of a product of elliptic curves.
  - M. Artebani, I. Dolgachev, The Hesse pencil of plane cubic curves,
    Enseign. Math. 55 (2009), 235-273.

RETURN CONVENTION:
  Most constructors return a list [R, I, type, description].  The ideal I lives
  in the returned ring R.  Use setring on the first entry before working with I.

EXAMPLE:
  LIB \"surface_type_generics.lib\";
  ring r=31991,(dummy),dp;
  list K=surface_generic_K3();
  setring K[1];
  ideal IK3=K[2];
  IK3;
";

LIB "matrix.lib";
LIB "elim.lib";

///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Internal utility functions
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

static proc surface_generic_default_bound(list #)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_default_bound([bound]);
RETURN:  an integer coefficient bound.
PURPOSE: Internal helper.  All random constructors use a small integer bound by
         default.  Over a finite field, the integers are interpreted in the
         coefficient field.  Over characteristic zero, they are rational integers.
EXAMPLE: ring r=0,(x),dp; surface_generic_default_bound();
"
{
  int bound=20;
  if(size(#)>0) { bound=#[1]; }
  if(bound<1) { bound=20; }
  return(bound);
}

static proc surface_generic_coeff(int bound)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_coeff(bound);
RETURN:  a non-zero random scalar represented by an integer.
PURPOSE: Internal helper for random forms.  Avoiding zero coefficients reduces
         the chance of accidentally leaving the intended open subset.
EXAMPLE: ring r=31991,(x),dp; surface_generic_coeff(10);
"
{
  int c=random(-bound,bound);
  if(c==0) { c=1; }
  return(c);
}

static proc surface_generic_unique_suffix()
"USAGE:   surface_generic_unique_suffix();
RETURN:  a short suffix for dynamically created ring names.
PURPOSE: Internal helper.  Constructors may be called several times in the same
         second, so timer alone is not enough to avoid redefinition warnings.
EXAMPLE: ring r=0,(x),dp; surface_generic_unique_suffix();
"
{
  return("_"+string(timer)+"_"+string(random(1,1000000000)));
}

static proc surface_generic_random_homogeneous(int d, int bound)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_random_homogeneous(d,bound); d>=0
RETURN:  a random homogeneous polynomial of degree d in all variables of the
         current ring.
MATHEMATICS:
         Smooth complete intersections and 1-generic determinantal models are
         open conditions on coefficients.  This helper samples the coefficient
         space of forms of a fixed degree.
EXAMPLE: ring r=31991,(x0,x1,x2,x3,x4),dp; surface_generic_random_homogeneous(2,10);
"
{
  ideal mons=maxideal(d);
  poly f=0;
  int i;
  for(i=1;i<=size(mons);i=i+1)
  {
    f=f+surface_generic_coeff(bound)*mons[i];
  }
  if(f==0) { f=mons[1]; }
  return(f);
}

static proc surface_generic_form3(poly A, poly B, poly C, int d, int bound)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_form3(A,B,C,d,bound);
RETURN:  a random homogeneous expression of degree d in A,B,C.
MATHEMATICS:
         Used for curves pulled back from the two factors of a Segre product.
         For example, f(column_j)=0 for every column cuts E x P^1 or E x F on
         a rank-one matrix parameterizing P^2 x P^1 or P^2 x P^2.
EXAMPLE: ring r=31991,(a,b,c),dp; surface_generic_form3(a,b,c,3,10);
"
{
  int i;
  int j;
  int k;
  poly f=0;
  for(i=0;i<=d;i=i+1)
  {
    for(j=0;j<=d-i;j=j+1)
    {
      k=d-i-j;
      f=f+surface_generic_coeff(bound)*A^i*B^j*C^k;
    }
  }
  return(f);
}

static proc surface_generic_hesse(poly A, poly B, poly C, number lam)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_hesse(A,B,C,lambda);
RETURN:  A^3+B^3+C^3-3*lambda*A*B*C.
MATHEMATICS:
         This is the Hesse pencil of plane cubic curves.  Away from the
         discriminant lambda^3=1, and in characteristic not 3 in this simple
         presentation, it gives a smooth elliptic curve.  The Hesse pencil is
         convenient because some torsion translations are linear projective
         transformations.
EXAMPLE: ring r=31991,(x,y,z),dp; surface_generic_hesse(x,y,z,2);
"
{
  poly f=A^3+B^3+C^3-3*lam*A*B*C;
  return(f);
}

static proc surface_generic_quartic3(poly A, poly B, poly C, int bound)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_quartic3(A,B,C,bound);
RETURN:  a random plane quartic expression in A,B,C.
MATHEMATICS:
         A smooth plane quartic has genus 3.  The product of such a curve with
         an elliptic curve is a smooth elliptic surface of Kodaira dimension 1.
         Smoothness is open in the quartic coefficient space.
EXAMPLE: ring r=31991,(x,y,z),dp; surface_generic_quartic3(x,y,z,10);
"
{
  return(surface_generic_form3(A,B,C,4,bound));
}


static proc surface_generic_quartic3_special(poly A, poly B, poly C, number a, number b, number c, number d)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_quartic3_special(A,B,C,a,b,c,d);
RETURN:  a four-parameter plane quartic in A,B,C.
MATHEMATICS:
         The same coefficients can be reused on all rows of a Segre matrix, so
         the equations really cut one plane quartic factor rather than three
         unrelated quartics.  Smoothness holds away from the discriminant.
EXAMPLE: ring r=31991,(x,y,z),dp; surface_generic_quartic3_special(x,y,z,1,2,3,4);
"
{
  poly f=A^4+B^4+C^4+a*A^2*B^2+b*A^2*C^2+c*B^2*C^2+d*A*B*C*(A+B+C);
  return(f);
}

static proc surface_generic_product_eliminate_vars(int n)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_product_eliminate_vars(n);
RETURN:  product var(1)*...*var(n) in the current ring.
PURPOSE: Internal helper for eliminate(..., product).  In the image-kernel
         computations, the first n variables are source variables to eliminate.
EXAMPLE: ring r=0,(a,b,c,x,y),dp; surface_generic_product_eliminate_vars(3);
"
{
  int i;
  poly p=1;
  for(i=1;i<=n;i=i+1)
  {
    p=p*var(i);
  }
  return(p);
}

static proc surface_generic_image_of_forms_mod_ideal(ideal Base, ideal Forms, string prefix)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_image_of_forms_mod_ideal(Base,Forms,prefix);
RETURN:  list [Rbig, J, FormsBig, description].  J is the kernel of the map
         k[prefix_i] -> k[source]/Base, prefix_i |-> Forms[i], represented in
         a ring containing both source and target variables.
MATHEMATICS:
         If Forms are homogeneous of the same degree and have no common base
         point on the relevant source after resolving the stated construction,
         Proj(k[Forms]) is the projective image.  For quotient constructions,
         Forms are invariant sections and Proj(k[Forms]) is a projective model
         of the quotient by the finite group action.
EXAMPLE: ring r=0,(s,t,u),dp; ideal B=0; ideal F=s2,t2,u2,s*t,s*u;
         list L=surface_generic_image_of_forms_mod_ideal(B,F,\"y\");
"
{
  def oldring=basering;
  ideal Bold=Base;
  ideal Fold=Forms;
  int n0=nvars(oldring);
  int m=size(Forms);
  string vars=varstr(oldring)+","+prefix+"(1.."+string(m)+")";
  string ringOrder="(dp("+string(n0)+"),dp("+string(m)+"))";
  string rname="surface_generic_image"+surface_generic_unique_suffix()+"_"+string(m);
  execute("ring "+rname+"="+charstr(oldring)+",("+vars+"),"+ringOrder+";");
  execute("def surface_generic_CREATED_RING="+rname+";");
  def Rbig=surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  kill surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  setring Rbig;
  ideal B=imap(oldring,Bold);
  ideal F=imap(oldring,Fold);
  ideal E=B;
  int i;
  for(i=1;i<=m;i=i+1)
  {
    E[size(E)+1]=var(n0+i)-F[i];
  }
  poly src=surface_generic_product_eliminate_vars(n0);
  ideal J=eliminate(E,src);
  list Out=Rbig,J,F,"image ideal by elimination; target variables are the last variables";
  execute("keepring "+rname+";");
  return(Out);
}

static proc surface_generic_segre_2x2_minors(matrix Z)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_segre_2x2_minors(Z); Z a matrix of variables
RETURN:  ideal of all 2x2 minors of Z.
MATHEMATICS:
         The 2x2 minors of a matrix of homogeneous coordinates cut out the
         Segre variety of rank-one matrices, such as P^2 x P^1 or P^2 x P^2.
EXAMPLE: ring r=0,(a,b,c,d,e,f),dp; matrix Z[3][2]=a,b,c,d,e,f;
         surface_generic_segre_2x2_minors(Z);
"
{
  return(minor(Z,2));
}

static proc surface_generic_same_type_note(string requested, string returned)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_same_type_note(requested,returned);
RETURN:  explanatory string for catalogue wrappers.
PURPOSE: Internal helper.  Some catalogue labels include embedded invariants;
         the wrappers in this library preserve the surface type, not necessarily
         the special P^4 Hilbert polynomial.
EXAMPLE: ring r=0,(x),dp; surface_generic_same_type_note(\"k3_d7_pi5\",\"K3\");
"
{
  string s="requested label: "+requested+"; returned a generic "+returned;
  s=s+" construction of the same Kodaira/minimal type; embedded degree and";
  s=s+" sectional genus are preserved only by the named direct constructors.";
  return(s);
}

static proc surface_generic_generators_of_degree(ideal I, int d)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_generators_of_degree(I,d);
RETURN:  the minimal generators of I having degree d.
PURPOSE: Internal helper for the graded pieces used in liaison constructions.
"
{
  ideal B=minbase(I);
  ideal D=ideal();
  int i;
  for(i=1;i<=size(B);i=i+1)
  {
    if(deg(B[i])==d) { D[size(D)+1]=B[i]; }
  }
  return(D);
}

static proc surface_generic_has_generator_below(ideal I, int d)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_has_generator_below(I,d);
RETURN:  1 if a minimal generator of I has degree less than d, and 0 otherwise.
PURPOSE: Internal guard ensuring that minimal degree-d generators span the full
         homogeneous component I_d used in a liaison step.
"
{
  ideal B=minbase(I);
  int i;
  for(i=1;i<=size(B);i=i+1)
  {
    if((B[i]!=0) and (deg(B[i])<d)) { return(1); }
  }
  return(0);
}

static proc surface_generic_same_ideal(ideal I, ideal J)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_same_ideal(I,J);
RETURN:  1 if I and J define the same ideal, and 0 otherwise.
PURPOSE: Internal two-sided standard-basis containment check.
"
{
  ideal GI=std(I);
  ideal GJ=std(J);
  int i;
  for(i=1;i<=size(GI);i=i+1)
  {
    if(reduce(GI[i],GJ)!=0) { return(0); }
  }
  for(i=1;i<=size(GJ);i=i+1)
  {
    if(reduce(GJ[i],GI)!=0) { return(0); }
  }
  return(1);
}

static proc surface_generic_saturated_ideal(ideal I)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_saturated_ideal(I);
RETURN:  the saturation of I by the irrelevant ideal.
PURPOSE: Internal helper for projective residual intersections.
"
{
  list L=sat(std(I),maxideal(1));
  return(std(L[1]));
}

static proc surface_generic_link(ideal completeIntersection, ideal component)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_link(completeIntersection,component);
RETURN:  the saturated ideal quotient defining the projective residual scheme.
PURPOSE: Internal helper for the Popescu--DES liaison construction.
"
{
  return(surface_generic_saturated_ideal(quotient(completeIntersection,component)));
}

static proc surface_generic_random_combination(ideal V, int bound)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_random_combination(V,bound);
RETURN:  a random scalar linear combination of the generators of V.
PURPOSE: Internal helper for choosing general members of a linear system.
"
{
  poly f=0;
  int i;
  for(i=1;i<=size(V);i=i+1)
  {
    f=f+surface_generic_coeff(bound)*V[i];
  }
  return(f);
}

static proc surface_generic_projectively_smooth(ideal I, int codimension)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_projectively_smooth(I,codimension);
RETURN:  1 if the Jacobian rank-drop locus is supported only at the vertex of
         the affine cone, and 0 otherwise.
PURPOSE: Internal projective Jacobian-criterion check.
"
{
  ideal B=minbase(I);
  ideal singularCone=std(I+minor(jacob(B),codimension));
  return(dim(singularCone)==0);
}

static proc surface_generic_prime_field_sqrt(number a, int p)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_prime_field_sqrt(a,p);
RETURN:  list [found,root] with root^2=a when found=1.
PURPOSE: Internal Tonelli--Shanks square root over the prime field F_p.
FIELD:   p must be an odd prime.
"
{
  if(a==0) { return(list(1,number(0))); }
  if(a^((p-1) div 2)!=1) { return(list(0,number(0))); }
  if((p%4)==3) { return(list(1,a^((p+1) div 4))); }

  int q=p-1;
  int s;
  while((q%2)==0)
  {
    q=q div 2;
    s++;
  }
  number z=2;
  while(z^((p-1) div 2)!=-1) { z=z+1; }
  number c=z^q;
  number x=a^((q+1) div 2);
  number t=a^q;
  int m=s;
  int i;
  int j;
  int exponent;
  number tt;
  number b;
  while(t!=1)
  {
    i=1;
    tt=t^2;
    while((i<m) and (tt!=1))
    {
      tt=tt^2;
      i++;
    }
    if(i==m) { return(list(0,number(0))); }
    exponent=1;
    for(j=1;j<=m-i-1;j=j+1) { exponent=2*exponent; }
    b=c^exponent;
    x=x*b;
    t=t*b^2;
    c=b^2;
    m=i;
  }
  return(list(1,x));
}

static proc surface_generic_elliptic_add(list P, list Q, number A)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_elliptic_add(P,Q,A);
RETURN:  P+Q on y^2=x^3+A*x+B.  A point is [infinity,x,y], with
         infinity=1 for the identity.
PURPOSE: Internal affine group law for choosing the five points in the
         Popescu--DES construction.
FIELD:   characteristic different from 2 and 3.
"
{
  if(P[1]!=0) { return(Q); }
  if(Q[1]!=0) { return(P); }
  number x1=P[2];
  number y1=P[3];
  number x2=Q[2];
  number y2=Q[3];
  if((x1==x2) and (y1+y2==0))
  {
    return(list(1,number(0),number(0)));
  }
  number lambda;
  if(x1==x2) { lambda=(3*x1^2+A)/(2*y1); }
  else { lambda=(y2-y1)/(x2-x1); }
  number x3=lambda^2-x1-x2;
  number y3=lambda*(x1-x3)-y1;
  return(list(0,x3,y3));
}

static proc surface_generic_same_elliptic_point(list P, list Q)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_same_elliptic_point(P,Q);
RETURN:  1 if the two internal elliptic-curve point records agree.
"
{
  if((P[1]!=0) or (Q[1]!=0)) { return((P[1]!=0) and (Q[1]!=0)); }
  return((P[2]==Q[2]) and (P[3]==Q[3]));
}

static proc surface_generic_random_elliptic_point(int p, number A, number B)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_random_elliptic_point(p,A,B);
RETURN:  an affine point on y^2=x^3+A*x+B over F_p.
PURPOSE: Internal bounded sampler used by the Popescu--DES construction.
FIELD:   the coefficient field must be the prime field F_p, p>3.
"
{
  int attempts;
  number x;
  number rhs;
  list root;
  number y;
  while(attempts<1000)
  {
    attempts++;
    x=random(0,p-1);
    rhs=x^3+A*x+B;
    root=surface_generic_prime_field_sqrt(rhs,p);
    if(root[1]==0) { continue; }
    y=root[2];
    if(random(0,1)==1) { y=-y; }
    return(list(0,x,y));
  }
  return(list(1,number(0),number(0)));
}

static proc surface_generic_DES_five_points(int p, number A, number B, number r)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_DES_five_points(p,A,B,r);
RETURN:  list [found,points], where points consists of five distinct affine
         points p_i on E with p_1+...+p_5=(r,0).
PURPOSE: Internal realization of the divisor condition in Popescu Section 3.8.
"
{
  list tau=0,r,number(0);
  int attempts;
  int i;
  int j;
  int duplicate;
  int valid;
  list points;
  list sum;
  list candidate;
  list negativeSum;
  while(attempts<100)
  {
    attempts++;
    points=list();
    sum=list(1,number(0),number(0));
    valid=1;
    for(i=1;i<=4;i=i+1)
    {
      candidate=surface_generic_random_elliptic_point(p,A,B);
      if((candidate[1]!=0) or surface_generic_same_elliptic_point(candidate,tau))
      {
        valid=0;
        break;
      }
      duplicate=0;
      for(j=1;j<i;j=j+1)
      {
        if(surface_generic_same_elliptic_point(candidate,points[j])) { duplicate=1; }
      }
      if(duplicate!=0)
      {
        valid=0;
        break;
      }
      points[i]=candidate;
      sum=surface_generic_elliptic_add(sum,candidate,A);
    }
    if(valid==0) { continue; }
    if(sum[1]!=0) { negativeSum=sum; }
    else { negativeSum=list(0,sum[2],-sum[3]); }
    points[5]=surface_generic_elliptic_add(tau,negativeSum,A);
    if((points[5][1]!=0) or surface_generic_same_elliptic_point(points[5],tau))
    {
      continue;
    }
    duplicate=0;
    for(j=1;j<=4;j=j+1)
    {
      if(surface_generic_same_elliptic_point(points[5],points[j])) { duplicate=1; }
    }
    if(duplicate==0) { return(list(1,points)); }
  }
  return(list(0,list()));
}

static proc surface_generic_DES_point_ideal(list points)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_DES_point_ideal(points);
RETURN:  the homogeneous ideal of five points [1:x:y:x^2:x*y] in P^4.
PURPOSE: Internal helper for the first (2,2) link in Popescu Section 3.8.
"
{
  if(nvars(basering)!=5)
  {
    ERROR("surface_generic_DES_point_ideal requires a five-variable ring");
  }
  ideal pointUnion=ideal(1);
  int i;
  matrix P[2][5];
  for(i=1;i<=5;i=i+1)
  {
    P[1,1]=var(1); P[1,2]=var(2); P[1,3]=var(3);
    P[1,4]=var(4); P[1,5]=var(5);
    P[2,1]=1;
    P[2,2]=points[i][2];
    P[2,3]=points[i][3];
    P[2,4]=points[i][2]^2;
    P[2,5]=points[i][2]*points[i][3];
    pointUnion=intersect(pointUnion,minor(P,2));
  }
  return(std(pointUnion));
}

static proc surface_generic_DES_translation_scroll(number A, number r)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_DES_translation_scroll(A,r);
RETURN:  the elliptic quintic translation scroll Q in the current P^4.
MATHEMATICS:
         E is y^2=x^3+A*x+B with B=-r^3-A*r and tau=(r,0).  The procedure
         eliminates x,y and the two line parameters from the span of P and
         P+tau.  Thus Q is the union of the corresponding secant lines.
FIELD:   a prime field of characteristic greater than 3.
"
{
  def targetRing=basering;
  string rname="surface_generic_DES_scroll"+surface_generic_unique_suffix();
  execute("ring "+rname+"="+charstr(targetRing)+",(stgx,stgy,stgu,stgv,stgz0,stgz1,stgz2,stgz3,stgz4),dp;");
  execute("def surface_generic_CREATED_RING="+rname+";");
  def scrollRing=surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  kill surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  setring scrollRing;
  number AA=imap(targetRing,A);
  number rr=imap(targetRing,r);
  number BB=-rr^3-AA*rr;
  poly D=var(1)-rr;
  poly N=rr*var(1)+2*rr^2+AA;
  number c=AA+3*rr^2;
  ideal graph=
    var(2)^2-var(1)^3-AA*var(1)-BB,
    var(5)-(var(3)+var(4)*D^3),
    var(6)-(var(3)*var(1)+var(4)*N*D^2),
    var(7)-(var(3)*var(2)-var(4)*c*var(2)*D),
    var(8)-(var(3)*var(1)^2+var(4)*N^2*D),
    var(9)-(var(3)*var(1)*var(2)-var(4)*c*N*var(2));
  ideal Qbig=minbase(eliminate(graph,surface_generic_product_eliminate_vars(4)));
  setring targetRing;
  map projectScroll=scrollRing,0,0,0,0,var(1),var(2),var(3),var(4),var(5);
  ideal Q=surface_generic_saturated_ideal(projectScroll(Qbig));
  return(Q);
}

static proc surface_generic_MB_raomodule(number a, number b)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_MB_raomodule(a,b);
RETURN:  the sum of the ideals of two elliptic normal quintics in the current
         five-variable ring.
PURPOSE: Internal helper for surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_MB_dim31.  The parameters a
         and b are the two points on the polar conic in the Moore normal form.
"
{
  if(nvars(basering)!=5)
  {
    ERROR("surface_generic_MB_raomodule requires a ring with five variables");
  }

  map sigma=basering,var(5),var(1),var(2),var(3),var(4);
  ideal M=ideal();
  poly q=var(3)*var(4)+a*var(1)^2-a^2*var(2)*var(5);
  int i;
  for(i=1;i<=5;i=i+1)
  {
    q=sigma(q);
    M[size(M)+1]=q;
  }
  q=var(3)*var(4)+b*var(1)^2-b^2*var(2)*var(5);
  for(i=1;i<=5;i=i+1)
  {
    q=sigma(q);
    M[size(M)+1]=q;
  }
  return(minbase(M));
}

static proc surface_generic_MB_split_polar_point(def rootRing, number bringA)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_MB_split_polar_point(rootRing,bringA);
RETURN:  list [found,bringC,polarA,polarB].  If found=1, (bringA:1:bringC)
         lies on the Bring curve and polarA,polarB are the two distinct roots
         of the corresponding polar quadratic.
PURPOSE: Internal univariate finite-field root extraction for the normalized
         Moore/Bring construction.  rootRing is a reusable one-variable ring;
         factoring there avoids multivariate factorization in the ambient P^4
         ring and avoids rebuilding a coefficient ring for every trial.
FIELD:   a prime field of characteristic different from 2 and 5.
"
{
  def targetRing=basering;
  setring rootRing;

  number a=imap(targetRing,bringA);
  poly bringPolynomial=a^4*var(1)-a^2*var(1)^2
                       -a*(1+var(1)^5)+2*var(1)^3;
  ideal bringFactors=factorize(bringPolynomial,1);
  poly polarPolynomial;
  ideal polarFactors;
  poly currentFactor;
  number bringC;
  number polarDiscriminant;
  number polarA;
  number polarB;
  int bringFactorIndex;
  int currentFactorIndex;
  int polarFactorIndex;
  int currentPolarFactorIndex;
  int rootCount;
  bringFactorIndex=1;
  while(bringFactorIndex<=size(bringFactors))
  {
    currentFactorIndex=bringFactorIndex;
    bringFactorIndex++;
    if(deg(bringFactors[currentFactorIndex])!=1) { continue; }
    bringC=-leadcoef(subst(bringFactors[currentFactorIndex],var(1),0))
           /leadcoef(bringFactors[currentFactorIndex]);
    if(bringC==0) { continue; }

    polarDiscriminant=a^2+4*bringC;
    if(polarDiscriminant==0) { continue; }

    polarPolynomial=-bringC*var(1)^2+a*var(1)+1;
    polarFactors=factorize(polarPolynomial,1);
    polarFactorIndex=1;
    rootCount=0;
    while(polarFactorIndex<=size(polarFactors))
    {
      currentPolarFactorIndex=polarFactorIndex;
      polarFactorIndex++;
      currentFactor=polarFactors[currentPolarFactorIndex];
      if(deg(currentFactor)==1)
      {
        rootCount++;
        if(rootCount==1)
        {
          polarA=-leadcoef(subst(currentFactor,var(1),0))
                 /leadcoef(currentFactor);
        }
        if(rootCount==2)
        {
          polarB=-leadcoef(subst(currentFactor,var(1),0))
                 /leadcoef(currentFactor);
        }
      }
    }
    if((rootCount<2) or (polarA==polarB)) { continue; }

    setring targetRing;
    number outputBringC=imap(rootRing,bringC);
    number outputPolarA=imap(rootRing,polarA);
    number outputPolarB=imap(rootRing,polarB);
    return(list(1,outputBringC,outputPolarA,outputPolarB));
  }

  setring targetRing;
  return(list(0,number(0),number(0),number(0)));
}

///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Direct generic surface-type constructors
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

proc surface_generic_info()
"USAGE:   surface_generic_info();
RETURN:  a short string describing the library.
MATHEMATICS:
         The library constructs generic representatives by geometric recipes:
         determinantal scrolls for rational ruled surfaces, complete
         intersections for K3 surfaces, products of curves for abelian/elliptic
         surfaces, free quotient data for Enriques and bielliptic surfaces, the
         full older 34-dimensional Decker--Ein--Schreyer degree-11 Enriques
         family, and its separate normalized Moore/Bring specialization.
EXAMPLE: surface_generic_info();
"
{
  string s="surface_type_generics.lib: stand-alone generic constructions for ";
  s=s+"rational, elliptic ruled, K3, Enriques, abelian, bielliptic, and ";
  s=s+"properly elliptic surface types.  Constructors return [ring,ideal,type,notes], ";
  s=s+"or quotient-cover data for finite quotient types.  The full older ";
  s=s+"34-dimensional degree-11 Enriques liaison family in P^4 and a separate ";
  s=s+"Moore/Bring specialization are included.";
  return(s);
}

proc surface_generic_surface_types()
"USAGE:   surface_generic_surface_types();
RETURN:  list of supported type names.
MATHEMATICS:
         These names correspond to the surface types in the Enriques-Kodaira
         classification which occur in the catalogue for non-general-type
         surfaces: rational/ruled, Kodaira dimension 0 types, and properly
         elliptic types.
EXAMPLE: list T=surface_generic_surface_types(); T;
"
{
  list T="rational","rational_scroll","projected_veronese","elliptic_ruled","K3","Enriques","abelian","bielliptic","elliptic";
  return(T);
}

proc surface_generic_cubic_scroll(list #)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_cubic_scroll([bound]);
RETURN:  list [R,I,\"rational\",description], where I is generated by the 2x2
         minors of a generic 2x3 matrix of linear forms in P^4.
MATHEMATICS:
         A 1-generic 2x3 matrix of linear forms on P^4 has rank-one locus a
         smooth cubic rational normal scroll.  The 1-generic condition is open;
         random coefficients give a generic member of this determinantal family.
EXAMPLE: ring r=31991,(dummy),dp; list S=surface_generic_cubic_scroll(); setring S[1]; S[2];
"
{
  int bound=surface_generic_default_bound(#);
  def oldring=basering;
  string rname="surface_generic_cubic_scroll_R"+surface_generic_unique_suffix();
  execute("ring "+rname+"="+charstr(oldring)+",(x0,x1,x2,x3,x4),dp;");
  execute("def surface_generic_CREATED_RING="+rname+";");
  def R=surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  kill surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  setring R;

  matrix M[2][3];
  int i;
  int j;
  for(i=1;i<=2;i=i+1)
  {
    for(j=1;j<=3;j=j+1)
    {
      M[i,j]=surface_generic_random_homogeneous(1,bound);
    }
  }
  ideal I=minor(M,2);
  list Out=R,I,"rational","generic cubic rational normal scroll in P^4 from 2x2 minors of a 1-generic 2x3 linear matrix";
  execute("keepring "+rname+";");
  return(Out);
}

proc surface_generic_projected_veronese(list #)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_projected_veronese([bound]);
RETURN:  list [Rbig,J,\"rational\",description].  J is an elimination ideal for
         the image of P^2 by five random conics.
MATHEMATICS:
         The complete conic system embeds P^2 as the Veronese surface in P^5.
         A general five-dimensional subsystem is projection from a point outside
         the secant hypersurface, hence gives a smooth projected Veronese surface
         in P^4.  Smoothness is an open condition on the chosen subsystem.
EXAMPLE: ring r=31991,(dummy),dp; list V=surface_generic_projected_veronese(); setring V[1]; V[2];
"
{
  int bound=surface_generic_default_bound(#);
  def oldring=basering;
  string rname="surface_generic_projected_veronese_R"+surface_generic_unique_suffix();
  execute("ring "+rname+"="+charstr(oldring)+",(s,t,u,y1,y2,y3,y4,y5),(dp(3),dp(5));");
  execute("def surface_generic_CREATED_RING="+rname+";");
  def Rbig=surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  kill surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  setring Rbig;
  ideal F;
  F[1]=s^2+surface_generic_coeff(bound)*t*u;
  F[2]=t^2+surface_generic_coeff(bound)*s*u;
  F[3]=u^2+surface_generic_coeff(bound)*s*t;
  F[4]=s*t+surface_generic_coeff(bound)*s*u;
  F[5]=t*u+surface_generic_coeff(bound)*s*u+surface_generic_coeff(bound)*s*t;
  ideal E;
  int i;
  for(i=1;i<=5;i=i+1)
  {
    E[i]=var(3+i)-F[i];
  }
  poly src=s*t*u;
  ideal J=eliminate(E,src);
  string tname="surface_generic_projected_veronese_target_R"+surface_generic_unique_suffix();
  execute("ring "+tname+"="+charstr(oldring)+",(y1,y2,y3,y4,y5),dp;");
  execute("def surface_generic_CREATED_RING="+tname+";");
  def Rt=surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  kill surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  setring Rt;
  ideal Jt=imap(Rbig,J);
  list Out=Rt,Jt,"rational","generic projected Veronese surface: image of P^2 by a general 5-dimensional subsystem of conics";
  execute("keepring "+tname+";");
  return(Out);
}

proc surface_generic_K3(list #)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_K3([bound]);
RETURN:  list [R,I,\"K3\",description], where I=(quadric,cubic) in P^4.
MATHEMATICS:
         A smooth complete intersection of type (2,3) in P^4 has trivial
         canonical bundle by adjunction, H^1(O)=0 by the Lefschetz/complete
         intersection cohomology calculation, and is a K3 surface.  Smoothness
         is open in the space of pairs (Q,C).
EXAMPLE: ring r=31991,(dummy),dp; list K=surface_generic_K3(); setring K[1]; K[2];
"
{
  int bound=surface_generic_default_bound(#);
  def oldring=basering;
  string rname="surface_generic_K3_R"+surface_generic_unique_suffix();
  execute("ring "+rname+"="+charstr(oldring)+",(x0,x1,x2,x3,x4),dp;");
  execute("def surface_generic_CREATED_RING="+rname+";");
  def R=surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  kill surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  setring R;
  poly Q=surface_generic_random_homogeneous(2,bound);
  poly C=surface_generic_random_homogeneous(3,bound);
  ideal I=Q,C;
  list Out=R,I,"K3","generic smooth complete intersection of a quadric and a cubic in P^4";
  execute("keepring "+rname+";");
  return(Out);
}

proc surface_generic_abelian_product(list #)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_abelian_product([lambda,mu]);
RETURN:  list [R,I,\"abelian\",description], where I defines E_lambda x E_mu
         in the Segre P^2 x P^2 subset P^8.
MATHEMATICS:
         A smooth plane cubic is an elliptic curve.  The product of two elliptic
         curves is an abelian surface.  The Segre equations impose rank one on a
         3x3 coordinate matrix; the column equations cut the first cubic and the
         row equations cut the second cubic.
EXAMPLE: ring r=31991,(dummy),dp; list A=surface_generic_abelian_product(2,5); setring A[1]; A[2];
"
{
  string lambdaString="2";
  string muString="5";
  if(size(#)>0) { lambdaString=string(#[1]); }
  if(size(#)>1) { muString=string(#[2]); }
  def oldring=basering;
  string rname="surface_generic_abelian_R"+surface_generic_unique_suffix();
  execute("ring "+rname+"="+charstr(oldring)+",(z00,z01,z02,z10,z11,z12,z20,z21,z22),dp;");
  execute("def surface_generic_CREATED_RING="+rname+";");
  def R=surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  kill surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  setring R;
  execute("number lambdaCoeff="+lambdaString+";");
  execute("number muCoeff="+muString+";");
  matrix Z[3][3]=z00,z01,z02,z10,z11,z12,z20,z21,z22;
  ideal I=surface_generic_segre_2x2_minors(Z);
  int j;
  for(j=1;j<=3;j=j+1)
  {
    I[size(I)+1]=surface_generic_hesse(Z[1,j],Z[2,j],Z[3,j],lambdaCoeff);
  }
  int i;
  for(i=1;i<=3;i=i+1)
  {
    I[size(I)+1]=surface_generic_hesse(Z[i,1],Z[i,2],Z[i,3],muCoeff);
  }
  list Out=R,I,"abelian","generic product abelian surface E_lambda x E_mu in the Segre embedding P^2 x P^2 -> P^8; choose lambda^3 and mu^3 different from 1";
  execute("keepring "+rname+";");
  return(Out);
}

proc surface_generic_elliptic_ruled(list #)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_elliptic_ruled([lambda]);
RETURN:  list [R,I,\"ruled\",description], defining E_lambda x P^1 in the
         Segre P^2 x P^1 subset P^5.
MATHEMATICS:
         The product E x P^1 is a geometrically ruled surface over an elliptic
         curve.  It has Kodaira dimension -infinity and irregularity 1.  The
         Segre rank-one equations give P^2 x P^1, and the cubic equation in
         every column cuts the elliptic curve factor.
EXAMPLE: ring r=31991,(dummy),dp; list S=surface_generic_elliptic_ruled(2); setring S[1]; S[2];
"
{
  string lambdaString="2";
  if(size(#)>0) { lambdaString=string(#[1]); }
  def oldring=basering;
  string rname="surface_generic_elliptic_ruled_R"+surface_generic_unique_suffix();
  execute("ring "+rname+"="+charstr(oldring)+",(z00,z01,z10,z11,z20,z21),dp;");
  execute("def surface_generic_CREATED_RING="+rname+";");
  def R=surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  kill surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  setring R;
  execute("number lambdaCoeff="+lambdaString+";");
  matrix Z[3][2]=z00,z01,z10,z11,z20,z21;
  ideal I=surface_generic_segre_2x2_minors(Z);
  I[size(I)+1]=surface_generic_hesse(Z[1,1],Z[2,1],Z[3,1],lambdaCoeff);
  I[size(I)+1]=surface_generic_hesse(Z[1,2],Z[2,2],Z[3,2],lambdaCoeff);
  list Out=R,I,"ruled","generic ruled surface E x P^1 over a smooth Hesse cubic E; same Kodaira type as elliptic scroll examples, not the special quintic P^4 embedding";
  execute("keepring "+rname+";");
  return(Out);
}

proc surface_generic_elliptic_surface(list #)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_elliptic_surface([lambda,bound]);
RETURN:  list [R,I,\"elliptic\",description], defining E_lambda x C where C is
         a random smooth plane quartic, inside the Segre P^2 x P^2 subset P^8.
MATHEMATICS:
         Projection to the genus-3 curve C gives an isotrivial elliptic fibration
         with fibre E.  Since kappa(E)=0 and kappa(C)=1, the product surface has
         Kodaira dimension 1.  Smoothness of the quartic is open.
EXAMPLE: ring r=31991,(dummy),dp; list E=surface_generic_elliptic_surface(2,10); setring E[1]; E[2];
"
{
  string lambdaString="2";
  int bound=20;
  if(size(#)>0) { lambdaString=string(#[1]); }
  if(size(#)>1) { bound=#[2]; }
  def oldring=basering;
  string rname="surface_generic_elliptic_surface_R"+surface_generic_unique_suffix();
  execute("ring "+rname+"="+charstr(oldring)+",(z00,z01,z02,z10,z11,z12,z20,z21,z22),dp;");
  execute("def surface_generic_CREATED_RING="+rname+";");
  def R=surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  kill surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  setring R;
  execute("number lambdaCoeff="+lambdaString+";");
  matrix Z[3][3]=z00,z01,z02,z10,z11,z12,z20,z21,z22;
  ideal I=surface_generic_segre_2x2_minors(Z);
  int j;
  for(j=1;j<=3;j=j+1)
  {
    I[size(I)+1]=surface_generic_hesse(Z[1,j],Z[2,j],Z[3,j],lambdaCoeff);
  }
  number a=surface_generic_coeff(bound);
  number b=surface_generic_coeff(bound);
  number c=surface_generic_coeff(bound);
  number d=surface_generic_coeff(bound);
  poly Q1=surface_generic_quartic3_special(Z[1,1],Z[1,2],Z[1,3],a,b,c,d);
  poly Q2=surface_generic_quartic3_special(Z[2,1],Z[2,2],Z[2,3],a,b,c,d);
  poly Q3=surface_generic_quartic3_special(Z[3,1],Z[3,2],Z[3,3],a,b,c,d);
  I[size(I)+1]=Q1;
  I[size(I)+1]=Q2;
  I[size(I)+1]=Q3;
  list Out=R,I,"elliptic","generic isotrivial properly elliptic surface E x C, with E a Hesse cubic and C a random smooth plane quartic";
  execute("keepring "+rname+";");
  return(Out);
}

proc surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_DES_dim34(list #)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_DES_dim34([bound,checkSmooth,maxTries]);
RETURN:  list [R,I,\"Enriques\",description].  The ideal I defines a member
         of the 34-dimensional Decker--Ein--Schreyer family in P^4, of degree
         11 and sectional genus 10.
PARAMETERS:
         bound is the coefficient bound for random choices (default 20);
         checkSmooth asks for Jacobian certificates for every smooth variety
         used in the linkage chain (default 1); maxTries bounds the number of
         complete random liaison attempts (default 20).  Failure is an error;
         the routine never substitutes a stored surface or another family.
MATHEMATICS:
         This implements Popescu's liaison completion of the original
         Decker--Ein--Schreyer construction.  Start with an elliptic normal
         quintic E and a nonzero two-torsion point tau.  Five distinct points
         p_i with sum tau and a general plane determine the chain

           plane <-(2,2)-> cubic scroll T <-(3,3)-> Bordiga surface B.

         The union of the lines <p,p+tau> is an elliptic quintic scroll Q.
         There is a unique quintic V through B union Q.  Linking B by (3,5)
         gives a degree-nine surface Z; linking Q union Z by (5,5) gives I.
         The output has five quintic and ten sextic minimal generators.  The
         quintics link it back to Q, whose ruling is the one-dimensional
         family of six-secant lines.  Popescu proves that a general smooth
         output is an Enriques surface blown up in six points and that this
         DES family is irreducible, unirational, and 34-dimensional.
FIELD:   The automatic point sampler is implemented over a prime field F_p
         with p>3.  It uses a short Weierstrass model and Tonelli--Shanks.
         The characteristic-zero family is obtained by the lifting argument
         in the references; this finite-field routine does not perform that
         lift.
REFERENCES:
         W. Decker, L. Ein, F.-O. Schreyer, Construction of surfaces in P^4,
         J. Algebraic Geometry 2 (1993), 185-237;
         S. Popescu, On smooth surfaces of degree >= 11 in P^4, thesis,
         Sections 3.7--3.8 and Appendix 11.A.
EXAMPLE:
  ring r=1019,(dummy),dp;
  list E=surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_DES_dim34(20,1,20);
  setring E[1];
  ideal I=E[2];
  dim(std(I)),mult(std(I)),hilbPoly(std(I));
"
{
  int bound=surface_generic_default_bound(#);
  int checkSmooth=1;
  int maxTries=20;
  if(size(#)>1) { checkSmooth=#[2]; }
  if(size(#)>2) { maxTries=#[3]; }
  if(maxTries<1) { maxTries=20; }

  int characteristic=char(basering);
  if(characteristic==0)
  {
    ERROR("surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_DES_dim34: the automatic liaison sampler requires a finite prime field");
  }
  if(npars(basering)!=0)
  {
    ERROR("surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_DES_dim34: the automatic liaison sampler requires a prime coefficient field");
  }
  if(characteristic<=3)
  {
    ERROR("surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_DES_dim34: the short Weierstrass construction requires characteristic greater than 3");
  }

  def oldring=basering;
  string rname="surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_DES_dim34_R"+surface_generic_unique_suffix();
  execute("ring "+rname+"="+charstr(oldring)+",(x0,x1,x2,x3,x4),dp;");
  execute("def surface_generic_CREATED_RING="+rname+";");
  def R=surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  kill surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  setring R;

  intvec hpTExpected=2,5,3;
  intvec hpBExpected=2,2,6;
  intvec hpQExpected=0,5,5;
  intvec hpZExpected=8,-7,9;
  intvec hpYExpected=8,-22,14;
  intvec hpSExpected=2,-7,11;
  int constructionFound;
  int constructionAttempts;
  int i;
  int quinticCount;
  int sexticCount;
  number A;
  number r;
  number WeierstrassB;
  number discriminant;
  list pointData;
  list points;
  ideal ellipticCurve;
  ideal pointIdeal;
  poly planeEquation1;
  poly planeEquation2;
  ideal plane;
  ideal planeAndPoints;
  ideal quadrics;
  poly quadric1;
  poly quadric2;
  ideal cubicScroll;
  ideal scrollCurveUnion;
  ideal cubicsThroughScrollCurve;
  poly cubic1;
  poly cubic2;
  ideal Bordiga;
  ideal ellipticScroll;
  ideal BordigaScrollUnion;
  ideal commonQuintics;
  poly uniqueQuintic;
  ideal BordigaCubics;
  poly BordigaCubic;
  ideal degreeNineSurface;
  ideal intersectionCurve;
  ideal linkageUnion;
  ideal unionQuintics;
  ideal surfaceIdeal;
  ideal minimalSurfaceIdeal;
  ideal surfaceQuintics;
  ideal residualScroll;
  ideal residualIntersection;

  while((constructionFound==0) and (constructionAttempts<maxTries))
  {
    constructionAttempts++;
    A=surface_generic_coeff(bound);
    r=surface_generic_coeff(bound);
    WeierstrassB=-r^3-A*r;
    discriminant=4*A^3+27*WeierstrassB^2;
    if(discriminant==0) { continue; }

    ellipticCurve=
      var(2)^2-var(1)*var(4),
      var(2)*var(3)-var(1)*var(5),
      var(3)*var(4)-var(2)*var(5),
      var(3)^2-var(2)*var(4)-A*var(1)*var(2)-WeierstrassB*var(1)^2,
      var(3)*var(5)-var(4)^2-A*var(1)*var(4)-WeierstrassB*var(1)*var(2);
    ellipticCurve=surface_generic_saturated_ideal(ellipticCurve);
    if(dim(ellipticCurve)!=2) { continue; }
    if(mult(ellipticCurve)!=5) { continue; }
    if(checkSmooth!=0)
    {
      if(surface_generic_projectively_smooth(ellipticCurve,3)==0) { continue; }
    }

    pointData=surface_generic_DES_five_points(characteristic,A,WeierstrassB,r);
    if(pointData[1]==0) { continue; }
    points=pointData[2];
    pointIdeal=surface_generic_DES_point_ideal(points);
    if((dim(pointIdeal)!=1) or (mult(pointIdeal)!=5)) { continue; }

    planeEquation1=surface_generic_random_homogeneous(1,bound);
    planeEquation2=surface_generic_random_homogeneous(1,bound);
    plane=std(ideal(planeEquation1,planeEquation2));
    if(dim(plane)!=3) { continue; }
    planeAndPoints=intersect(plane,pointIdeal);
    if(surface_generic_has_generator_below(planeAndPoints,2)!=0) { continue; }
    quadrics=surface_generic_generators_of_degree(planeAndPoints,2);
    if(size(quadrics)!=4) { continue; }
    quadric1=surface_generic_random_combination(quadrics,bound);
    quadric2=surface_generic_random_combination(quadrics,bound);
    cubicScroll=surface_generic_link(ideal(quadric1,quadric2),plane);
    if((dim(cubicScroll)!=3) or (mult(cubicScroll)!=3)) { continue; }
    if(hilbPoly(cubicScroll)!=hpTExpected) { continue; }
    if(checkSmooth!=0)
    {
      if(surface_generic_projectively_smooth(cubicScroll,2)==0) { continue; }
    }
    if((dim(std(cubicScroll+ellipticCurve))!=1)
       or (mult(std(cubicScroll+ellipticCurve))!=5)) { continue; }

    scrollCurveUnion=intersect(cubicScroll,ellipticCurve);
    if(surface_generic_has_generator_below(scrollCurveUnion,3)!=0) { continue; }
    cubicsThroughScrollCurve=surface_generic_generators_of_degree(scrollCurveUnion,3);
    if(size(cubicsThroughScrollCurve)!=3) { continue; }
    cubic1=surface_generic_random_combination(cubicsThroughScrollCurve,bound);
    cubic2=surface_generic_random_combination(cubicsThroughScrollCurve,bound);
    Bordiga=surface_generic_link(ideal(cubic1,cubic2),cubicScroll);
    if((dim(Bordiga)!=3) or (mult(Bordiga)!=6)) { continue; }
    if(hilbPoly(Bordiga)!=hpBExpected) { continue; }
    if(surface_generic_has_generator_below(Bordiga,3)!=0) { continue; }
    if(size(surface_generic_generators_of_degree(Bordiga,3))!=4) { continue; }
    if(checkSmooth!=0)
    {
      if(surface_generic_projectively_smooth(Bordiga,2)==0) { continue; }
    }
    if((dim(std(Bordiga+ellipticCurve))!=2)
       or (mult(std(Bordiga+ellipticCurve))!=5)) { continue; }

    ellipticScroll=surface_generic_DES_translation_scroll(A,r);
    if((dim(ellipticScroll)!=3) or (mult(ellipticScroll)!=5)) { continue; }
    if(hilbPoly(ellipticScroll)!=hpQExpected) { continue; }
    if(surface_generic_has_generator_below(ellipticScroll,3)!=0) { continue; }
    if(size(surface_generic_generators_of_degree(ellipticScroll,3))!=5) { continue; }
    if(checkSmooth!=0)
    {
      if(surface_generic_projectively_smooth(ellipticScroll,2)==0) { continue; }
    }
    if((dim(std(ellipticScroll+ellipticCurve))!=2)
       or (mult(std(ellipticScroll+ellipticCurve))!=5)) { continue; }

    BordigaScrollUnion=intersect(Bordiga,ellipticScroll);
    if(surface_generic_has_generator_below(BordigaScrollUnion,5)!=0) { continue; }
    commonQuintics=surface_generic_generators_of_degree(BordigaScrollUnion,5);
    if(size(commonQuintics)!=1) { continue; }
    uniqueQuintic=commonQuintics[1];
    BordigaCubics=surface_generic_generators_of_degree(Bordiga,3);
    if(size(BordigaCubics)!=4) { continue; }
    BordigaCubic=surface_generic_random_combination(BordigaCubics,bound);
    degreeNineSurface=surface_generic_link(ideal(uniqueQuintic,BordigaCubic),Bordiga);
    if((dim(degreeNineSurface)!=3) or (mult(degreeNineSurface)!=9)) { continue; }
    if(hilbPoly(degreeNineSurface)!=hpZExpected) { continue; }
    if(checkSmooth!=0)
    {
      if(surface_generic_projectively_smooth(degreeNineSurface,2)==0) { continue; }
    }

    intersectionCurve=std(degreeNineSurface+ellipticScroll);
    if((dim(intersectionCurve)!=2) or (mult(intersectionCurve)!=10)) { continue; }
    if(checkSmooth!=0)
    {
      if(surface_generic_projectively_smooth(intersectionCurve,3)==0) { continue; }
    }
    linkageUnion=intersect(ellipticScroll,degreeNineSurface);
    if((dim(std(linkageUnion))!=3) or (mult(std(linkageUnion))!=14)) { continue; }
    if(hilbPoly(std(linkageUnion))!=hpYExpected) { continue; }
    if(surface_generic_has_generator_below(linkageUnion,5)!=0) { continue; }
    unionQuintics=surface_generic_generators_of_degree(linkageUnion,5);
    if(size(unionQuintics)!=2) { continue; }
    surfaceIdeal=surface_generic_link(ideal(unionQuintics[1],unionQuintics[2]),linkageUnion);
    if((dim(surfaceIdeal)!=3) or (mult(surfaceIdeal)!=11)) { continue; }
    if(hilbPoly(surfaceIdeal)!=hpSExpected) { continue; }

    minimalSurfaceIdeal=minbase(surfaceIdeal);
    if((size(minimalSurfaceIdeal)!=15)
       or (surface_generic_has_generator_below(minimalSurfaceIdeal,5)!=0)) { continue; }
    quinticCount=0;
    sexticCount=0;
    surfaceQuintics=ideal();
    for(i=1;i<=size(minimalSurfaceIdeal);i=i+1)
    {
      if(deg(minimalSurfaceIdeal[i])==5)
      {
        quinticCount++;
        surfaceQuintics[size(surfaceQuintics)+1]=minimalSurfaceIdeal[i];
      }
      if(deg(minimalSurfaceIdeal[i])==6) { sexticCount++; }
    }
    if((quinticCount!=5) or (sexticCount!=10)) { continue; }
    residualScroll=surface_generic_saturated_ideal(quotient(surfaceQuintics,surfaceIdeal));
    if((dim(residualScroll)!=3) or (mult(residualScroll)!=5)) { continue; }
    if(hilbPoly(residualScroll)!=hpQExpected) { continue; }
    if(surface_generic_same_ideal(residualScroll,ellipticScroll)==0) { continue; }
    residualIntersection=std(residualScroll+surfaceIdeal);
    if((dim(residualIntersection)!=2) or (mult(residualIntersection)!=20)) { continue; }
    if(checkSmooth!=0)
    {
      if(surface_generic_projectively_smooth(surfaceIdeal,2)==0) { continue; }
    }
    constructionFound=1;
  }
  if(constructionFound==0)
  {
    ERROR("surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_DES_dim34: no valid Popescu--DES liaison member found within maxTries");
  }

  attrib(surfaceIdeal,"construction","Popescu liaison completion of the Decker--Ein--Schreyer Enriques family");
  attrib(surfaceIdeal,"familyDimension",34);
  attrib(surfaceIdeal,"degree",11);
  attrib(surfaceIdeal,"sectionalGenus",10);
  attrib(surfaceIdeal,"constructionAttempts",constructionAttempts);
  attrib(surfaceIdeal,"smoothnessChecked",checkSmooth!=0);
  attrib(surfaceIdeal,"residualScrollDegree",5);
  string description="random member of the irreducible unirational 34-dimensional Decker--Ein--Schreyer family in P^4, constructed by Popescu's liaison chain; degree 11, sectional genus 10, five quintic and ten sextic minimal generators, with an elliptic quintic scroll residual to the quintics";
  if(checkSmooth!=0)
  {
    description=description+"; all smoothness hypotheses and the final surface were certified by the Jacobian criterion";
  }
  else
  {
    description=description+"; smoothness was not checked";
  }
  list Out=R,surfaceIdeal,"Enriques",description;
  execute("keepring "+rname+";");
  return(Out);
}

proc surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_MB_dim31(list #)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_MB_dim31([bound,checkSmooth,maxTries]);
RETURN:  list [R,I,\"Enriques\",description].  The ideal I defines a surface
         in P^4 of degree 11 and sectional genus 10.
PARAMETERS:
         bound is the coefficient bound for the random constant 5x2 matrix
         (default 20); checkSmooth asks the constructor to certify projective
         smoothness and retry if necessary (default 1); maxTries bounds the
         random determinantal-map trials (default 20).
MATHEMATICS:
         This is a normalized Moore/Bring specialization inside the older DES
         construction, not a parametrization of its full 34-dimensional family.
         A point on the Bring curve determines a polar line to the Moore conic;
         its two intersection points parameterize two elliptic normal quintics
         on a common elliptic quintic scroll.  Their ideal sum presents the
         finite-length Rao module S/M with Hilbert function (1,5,5).  The
         procedure extracts its five extra degree-four third syzygies and
         performs Schreyer's double-syzygy construction.  Equivalently, it uses
             2*Omega^3_P4(3) -> ker(10*O_P4 -> O_P4(2)).
         The resulting ideal has five quintic and ten sextic minimal generators.
         A smooth member is an Enriques surface blown up in six points.  The
         tangent computation for this normalized module locus gives dimension
         25; the choice Gr(2,5) adds 6, so this construction is locally
         incomplete and has image dimension at most 31.  Use
         surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_DES_dim34 for the full older 34-dimensional liaison
         family.
FIELD:   The implemented random parameter search is for prime fields of
         characteristic different from 2 and 5.  Very small fields may not
         contain a usable split-polar specialization within the bounded point
         search.
REFERENCES:
         W. Decker, L. Ein, F.-O. Schreyer, Construction of surfaces in P^4,
         J. Algebraic Geometry 2 (1993), 185-237;
         S. Popescu, On smooth surfaces of degree >= 11 in P^4, thesis,
         Sections 3.7--3.8;
         H. Abo, K. Ranestad, F.-O. Schreyer,
         NongeneralTypeSurfacesInP4.m2 (2026), procedures
         specialEnriquesSchreyerSurface and schreyerSurfaceFromModule.
EXAMPLE:
  ring r=1009,(dummy),dp;
  list E=surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_MB_dim31(20,0);
  setring E[1];
  ideal I=E[2];
  dim(std(I)),mult(std(I)),hilbPoly(std(I));
"
{
  int bound=surface_generic_default_bound(#);
  int checkSmooth=1;
  int maxTries=20;
  if(size(#)>1) { checkSmooth=#[2]; }
  if(size(#)>2) { maxTries=#[3]; }
  if(maxTries<1) { maxTries=20; }

  int characteristic=char(basering);
  if(characteristic==0)
  {
    ERROR("surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_MB_dim31: the automatic Bring-curve search requires a finite coefficient field");
  }
  if(npars(basering)!=0)
  {
    ERROR("surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_MB_dim31: the automatic Bring-curve search requires a prime coefficient field");
  }
  if((characteristic==2) or (characteristic==5))
  {
    ERROR("surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_MB_dim31: characteristics 2 and 5 are not supported by this Moore/Bring-curve construction");
  }

  def oldring=basering;
  string rname="surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_MB_dim31_R"+surface_generic_unique_suffix();
  execute("ring "+rname+"="+charstr(oldring)+",(x0,x1,x2,x3,x4),dp;");
  execute("def surface_generic_CREATED_RING="+rname+";");
  def R=surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  kill surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  setring R;

  string rootRingName=rname+"_roots";
  execute("ring "+rootRingName+"="+charstr(R)+",(t),dp;");
  execute("def surface_generic_CREATED_RING="+rootRingName+";");
  def rootRing=surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  kill surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  setring R;

  // A point (bringA:1:bringC) on the Bring sextic determines a polar
  // line to u0^2+4*u1*u2.  Its two intersection points with that conic
  // are the Moore parameters polarA and polarB for the elliptic quintics.
  number bringA;
  number bringC;
  number polarA;
  number polarB;
  list polarPointData;
  ideal M=ideal();
  ideal GM;
  int pointFound;
  int pointAttempts;
  int pointLimit=10*maxTries;
  if(pointLimit<100) { pointLimit=100; }
  int i;

  while((pointFound==0) and (pointAttempts<pointLimit))
  {
    pointAttempts++;
    bringA=random(1,characteristic-1);
    polarPointData=surface_generic_MB_split_polar_point(rootRing,bringA);
    if(polarPointData[1]==0) { continue; }
    bringC=polarPointData[2];
    polarA=polarPointData[3];
    polarB=polarPointData[4];

    M=surface_generic_MB_raomodule(polarA,polarB);
    GM=std(M);
    if((size(M)==10) and (dim(GM)==0) and (vdim(GM)==11))
    {
      pointFound=1;
    }
  }
  if(pointFound==0)
  {
    ERROR("surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_MB_dim31: no usable split-polar Bring-curve point found; use a larger finite field or increase maxTries");
  }

  // The resolution of S/M has Betti totals 1,10,25,31,20,5.  Select
  // summands by their homogeneous shifts, never by accidental column order.
  resolution FM=mres(M,0);
  if(size(FM)<4)
  {
    ERROR("surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_MB_dim31: the sampled Rao module has the wrong resolution length");
  }
  matrix d2=FM[2];
  matrix d3=FM[3];
  intvec shiftsF2=attrib(FM[3],"isHomog");
  intvec shiftsF3=attrib(FM[4],"isHomog");
  intvec degreeThreeRows;
  intvec degreeFourColumns;
  int numberDegreeThree;
  int numberDegreeFour;
  for(i=1;i<=size(shiftsF2);i=i+1)
  {
    if(shiftsF2[i]==3)
    {
      numberDegreeThree++;
      degreeThreeRows[numberDegreeThree]=i;
    }
  }
  for(i=1;i<=size(shiftsF3);i=i+1)
  {
    if(shiftsF3[i]==4)
    {
      numberDegreeFour++;
      degreeFourColumns[numberDegreeFour]=i;
    }
  }
  if((numberDegreeThree!=15) or (numberDegreeFour!=5))
  {
    ERROR("surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_MB_dim31: the sampled Rao module does not have the required 15-by-5 linear syzygy block");
  }

  matrix N=submat(d3,degreeThreeRows,degreeFourColumns);
  matrix d2Linear=submat(d2,1..nrows(d2),degreeThreeRows);
  matrix C[ncols(N)][2];
  matrix N2;
  matrix m10;
  matrix equations;
  module imageN2;
  module relationsN2;
  module constantRelations;
  module doubleRelations;
  module finalRelations;
  ideal X=ideal();
  ideal GX;
  ideal singularCone;
  intvec hp;
  intvec expectedHP=2,-7,11;
  int surfaceFound;
  int surfaceAttempts;
  int constantCount;
  int quintics;
  int sextics;

  while((surfaceFound==0) and (surfaceAttempts<maxTries))
  {
    surfaceAttempts++;
    for(i=1;i<=nrows(C);i=i+1)
    {
      C[i,1]=surface_generic_coeff(bound);
      C[i,2]=surface_generic_coeff(bound);
    }
    N2=N*C;
    imageN2=std(module(transpose(N2)));
    if(dim(imageN2)!=0) { continue; }

    relationsN2=syz(module(transpose(N2)));
    constantRelations=module();
    constantCount=0;
    for(i=1;i<=size(relationsN2);i=i+1)
    {
      if(deg(relationsN2[i])==0)
      {
        constantCount++;
        constantRelations[constantCount]=relationsN2[i];
      }
    }
    if(constantCount!=5) { continue; }

    doubleRelations=syz(module(transpose(matrix(constantRelations))));
    if((nrows(matrix(doubleRelations))!=15)
       or (ncols(matrix(doubleRelations))!=10)) { continue; }
    m10=d2Linear*matrix(doubleRelations);
    if((nrows(m10)!=10) or (ncols(m10)!=10)) { continue; }

    finalRelations=syz(module(transpose(m10)));
    if((nrows(matrix(finalRelations))!=10)
       or (ncols(matrix(finalRelations))!=2)) { continue; }
    equations=transpose(matrix(finalRelations))*d2;
    X=minbase(ideal(equations));
    if(size(X)!=15) { continue; }

    quintics=0;
    sextics=0;
    for(i=1;i<=size(X);i=i+1)
    {
      if(deg(X[i])==5) { quintics++; }
      if(deg(X[i])==6) { sextics++; }
    }
    if((quintics!=5) or (sextics!=10)) { continue; }

    GX=std(X);
    if((dim(GX)!=3) or (mult(GX)!=11)) { continue; }
    hp=hilbPoly(GX);
    if(hp!=expectedHP) { continue; }

    if(checkSmooth!=0)
    {
      singularCone=std(X+minor(jacob(X),2));
      if(dim(singularCone)!=0) { continue; }
    }
    surfaceFound=1;
  }
  if(surfaceFound==0)
  {
    ERROR("surface_generic_enriques_d11_pi10_MB_dim31: no valid determinantal map found within maxTries");
  }

  attrib(X,"construction","Moore--Bring specialization of the Decker--Ein--Schreyer Enriques construction");
  attrib(X,"constructionDimensionUpperBound",31);
  attrib(X,"ambientDESFamilyDimension",34);
  attrib(X,"degree",11);
  attrib(X,"sectionalGenus",10);
  attrib(X,"smoothnessChecked",checkSmooth!=0);
  string description="random member of the locally incomplete Moore/Bring specialization of the Decker--Ein--Schreyer construction in P^4 (construction image of dimension at most 31 inside the older 34-dimensional family); degree 11, sectional genus 10, and five quintic plus ten sextic minimal generators";
  if(checkSmooth!=0)
  {
    description=description+"; projective smoothness certified by the Jacobian criterion";
  }
  else
  {
    description=description+"; projective smoothness was not checked";
  }
  list Out=R,X,"Enriques",description;
  execute("keepring "+rname+";");
  return(Out);
}

proc surface_generic_Enriques_cover(list #)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_Enriques_cover([bound]);
RETURN:  list [R,I,\"Enriques-cover\",involution,description].  I is a K3 cover
         in P^5 cut out by three invariant quadrics.
MATHEMATICS:
         Let P^5 have coordinates x0,x1,x2,y0,y1,y2 and let iota fix the x's
         and negate the y's.  Three general invariant quadrics q_i(x)+r_i(y)
         cut out a smooth K3 complete intersection.  For a non-empty open set of
         such triples, the cover avoids the two fixed P^2's of iota, so iota is
         fixed-point-free on the K3 surface.  The quotient is then an Enriques
         surface in characteristic not 2.
EXAMPLE: ring r=31991,(dummy),dp; list C=surface_generic_Enriques_cover(); setring C[1]; C[2];
"
{
  int bound=surface_generic_default_bound(#);
  def oldring=basering;
  string rname="surface_generic_Enriques_cover_R"+surface_generic_unique_suffix();
  execute("ring "+rname+"="+charstr(oldring)+",(x0,x1,x2,y0,y1,y2),dp;");
  execute("def surface_generic_CREATED_RING="+rname+";");
  def R=surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  kill surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  setring R;
  poly q1=surface_generic_form3(x0,x1,x2,2,bound)+surface_generic_form3(y0,y1,y2,2,bound);
  poly q2=surface_generic_form3(x0,x1,x2,2,bound)+surface_generic_form3(y0,y1,y2,2,bound);
  poly q3=surface_generic_form3(x0,x1,x2,2,bound)+surface_generic_form3(y0,y1,y2,2,bound);
  ideal I=q1,q2,q3;
  string inv="iota: (x0,x1,x2,y0,y1,y2) -> (x0,x1,x2,-y0,-y1,-y2)";
  list Out=R,I,"Enriques-cover",inv,"quotient of this K3 cover by the displayed fixed-point-free involution is a generic Enriques-type surface, in characteristic not 2";
  execute("keepring "+rname+";");
  return(Out);
}

proc surface_generic_Enriques_quotient_image(list #)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_Enriques_quotient_image([bound]);
RETURN:  list [Rbig,J,\"Enriques\",description].  J is an elimination ideal for
         an invariant-quadratic projective model of the quotient.
MATHEMATICS:
         This refines surface_generic_Enriques_cover.  The quotient by y -> -y is mapped by
         the invariant quadrics x_i x_j and y_i y_j.  Eliminating the K3-cover
         variables gives the ideal of the image of the quotient under this
         invariant linear system.  The computation is slower than returning the
         cover data but is a genuine projective quotient model.
EXAMPLE: ring r=31991,(dummy),dp; list E=surface_generic_Enriques_quotient_image(5); setring E[1]; E[2];
"
{
  int bound=surface_generic_default_bound(#);
  def oldring=basering;
  string rname="surface_generic_Enriques_quotient_R"+surface_generic_unique_suffix();
  execute("ring "+rname+"="+charstr(oldring)+",(x0,x1,x2,y0,y1,y2,u1,u2,u3,u4,u5,u6,u7,u8,u9,u10,u11,u12),(dp(6),dp(12));");
  execute("def surface_generic_CREATED_RING="+rname+";");
  def Rbig=surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  kill surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  setring Rbig;
  poly q1=surface_generic_form3(x0,x1,x2,2,bound)+surface_generic_form3(y0,y1,y2,2,bound);
  poly q2=surface_generic_form3(x0,x1,x2,2,bound)+surface_generic_form3(y0,y1,y2,2,bound);
  poly q3=surface_generic_form3(x0,x1,x2,2,bound)+surface_generic_form3(y0,y1,y2,2,bound);
  ideal I=q1,q2,q3;
  ideal F;
  F[1]=x0^2; F[2]=x0*x1; F[3]=x0*x2; F[4]=x1^2; F[5]=x1*x2; F[6]=x2^2;
  F[7]=y0^2; F[8]=y0*y1; F[9]=y0*y2; F[10]=y1^2; F[11]=y1*y2; F[12]=y2^2;
  ideal E=I;
  int i;
  for(i=1;i<=12;i=i+1)
  {
    E[size(E)+1]=var(6+i)-F[i];
  }
  poly src=x0*x1*x2*y0*y1*y2;
  ideal J=eliminate(E,src);
  string tname="surface_generic_Enriques_quotient_target_R"+surface_generic_unique_suffix();
  execute("ring "+tname+"="+charstr(oldring)+",(u1,u2,u3,u4,u5,u6,u7,u8,u9,u10,u11,u12),dp;");
  execute("def surface_generic_CREATED_RING="+tname+";");
  def Rt=surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  kill surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  setring Rt;
  ideal Jt=imap(Rbig,J);
  list Out=Rt,Jt,"Enriques","projective quotient image of a generic Enriques K3 cover by invariant quadrics; characteristic not 2";
  execute("keepring "+tname+";");
  return(Out);
}

proc surface_generic_bielliptic_cover_data(list #)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_bielliptic_cover_data([lambda,mu]);
RETURN:  list [R,I,\"bielliptic-cover\",action,description], where I defines
         E_lambda x F_mu in P^8 and action describes a free cyclic quotient.
MATHEMATICS:
         A bielliptic, also called hyperelliptic in the surface-classification
         literature, surface is a free finite quotient of a product of elliptic
         curves.  In the Hesse pencil, the cyclic permutation of coordinates is
         a translation by a 3-torsion point on a smooth member, hence has no
         fixed points.  Combining this free translation on the first factor with
         an order-3 automorphism of the second factor gives a free diagonal
         action on the product.  The quotient is bielliptic in characteristic
         not 3; for explicit invariant equations one should work over a field
         containing a primitive cube root of unity.
EXAMPLE: ring r=31991,(dummy),dp; list B=surface_generic_bielliptic_cover_data(2,5); setring B[1]; B[2];
"
{
  string lambdaString="2";
  string muString="5";
  if(size(#)>0) { lambdaString=string(#[1]); }
  if(size(#)>1) { muString=string(#[2]); }
  def oldring=basering;
  string rname="surface_generic_bielliptic_cover_R"+surface_generic_unique_suffix();
  execute("ring "+rname+"="+charstr(oldring)+",(z00,z01,z02,z10,z11,z12,z20,z21,z22),dp;");
  execute("def surface_generic_CREATED_RING="+rname+";");
  def R=surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  kill surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  setring R;
  execute("number lambdaCoeff="+lambdaString+";");
  execute("number muCoeff="+muString+";");
  matrix Z[3][3]=z00,z01,z02,z10,z11,z12,z20,z21,z22;
  ideal I=surface_generic_segre_2x2_minors(Z);
  int j;
  for(j=1;j<=3;j=j+1)
  {
    I[size(I)+1]=surface_generic_hesse(Z[1,j],Z[2,j],Z[3,j],lambdaCoeff);
  }
  int i;
  for(i=1;i<=3;i=i+1)
  {
    I[size(I)+1]=surface_generic_hesse(Z[i,1],Z[i,2],Z[i,3],muCoeff);
  }
  string action="g: first Hesse factor (x0,x1,x2)->(x1,x2,x0); second factor an order-3 automorphism with quotient P^1, e.g. diagonal (u0,u1,u2)->(u0,omega*u1,omega^2*u2) over a field with omega^2+omega+1=0";
  list Out=R,I,"bielliptic-cover",action,"the quotient of this product of elliptic curves by the displayed free cyclic action is a generic bielliptic/hyperelliptic-type surface; characteristic not 3";
  execute("keepring "+rname+";");
  return(Out);
}

proc surface_generic_rational_surface(list #)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_rational_surface([bound]);
RETURN:  list [R,I,\"rational\",description].
MATHEMATICS:
         This is the default generic rational representative: a cubic scroll in
         P^4.  Many rational examples in special catalogues are blow-ups of P^2
         embedded by special linear systems; the minimal Kodaira type is already
         represented by this generic scroll family.
EXAMPLE: ring r=31991,(dummy),dp; list R=surface_generic_rational_surface(); setring R[1]; R[2];
"
{
  int bound=surface_generic_default_bound(#);
  def oldring=basering;
  string rname="surface_generic_rational_R"+surface_generic_unique_suffix();
  execute("ring "+rname+"="+charstr(oldring)+",(x0,x1,x2,x3,x4),dp;");
  execute("def surface_generic_CREATED_RING="+rname+";");
  def R=surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  kill surface_generic_CREATED_RING;
  setring R;
  matrix M[2][3];
  int i;
  int j;
  for(i=1;i<=2;i=i+1)
  {
    for(j=1;j<=3;j=j+1)
    {
      M[i,j]=surface_generic_random_homogeneous(1,bound);
    }
  }
  ideal I=minor(M,2);
  list Out=R,I,"rational","generic rational representative: a cubic rational normal scroll in P^4";
  execute("keepring "+rname+";");
  return(Out);
}

proc surface_generic_genericSurface(string surfaceType, list #)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_genericSurface(type[,parameters]);
RETURN:  no return; use a direct constructor instead.
MATHEMATICS:
         This legacy dispatcher for constructors returning [ring,ideal,...]
         lists is disabled. Use
         surface_generic_K3, surface_generic_cubic_scroll, surface_generic_abelian_product, and the other direct
         constructors instead.
EXAMPLE: surface_generic_surface_types();
"
{
  ERROR("surface_generic_genericSurface cannot safely dispatch ring-returning constructors; use the direct surface_generic_* constructor for the requested type");
}

///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Catalogue wrappers.  They preserve surface type, not necessarily embedded
// degree and sectional genus.  Direct constructors preserve the standard simple
// model: cubic scroll, projected Veronese, K3 (2,3), product models, and quotient
// data.  The degree-11, genus-10 Enriques label has its full older
// 34-dimensional liaison construction above, together with a separate
// Moore/Bring syzygy specialization; most other high-degree P^4 labels are
// represented here only by generic models of the same Kodaira type.
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

proc surface_generic_catalogueNames()
"USAGE:   surface_generic_catalogueNames();
RETURN:  list of supported catalogue labels.
MATHEMATICS:
         The labels are grouped by surface type. Use a direct generic constructor
         for a random representative of that type. For the actual stored ideal,
         load nonGeneralTypeSurfacesP4.lib and call nonGeneralTypeSurfaceP4(name).
EXAMPLE: list N=surface_generic_catalogueNames(); N[1];
"
{
  list L="cubic_scroll","veronese","castelnuovo","bordiga","rational_d7_pi4","rational_d8_pi5","rational_d8_pi6","rational_d9_pi6","rational_d9_pi7","rational_d10_pi8","rational_d10_pi9_quart_1","rational_d10_pi9_quart_2","rational_d11_pi11_ss_0","rational_d11_pi11_ss_1","rational_d11_pi11_ss_inf","quintic_elliptic_scroll","enriques_d9_pi6","enriques_d10_pi8","enriques_d11_pi10","enriques_d13_pi16","enriques_d13_pi16_two","k3_d7_pi5","k3_d8_pi6","k3_d9_pi8","k3_d10_pi9_quart_1","k3_d10_pi9_quart_2","k3_d11_pi11_ss_0","k3_d11_pi11_ss_1","k3_d11_pi11_ss_2","k3_d11_pi11_ss_3","k3_d11_pi12","k3_d12_pi14","k3_d13_pi16","k3_d14_pi19","bielliptic_d10_pi6","bielliptic_d15_pi21","abelian_d10_pi6","abelian_d15_pi21_quintic_3","abelian_d15_pi21_quintic_1","elliptic_d7_pi6","elliptic_d8_pi7","elliptic_d9_pi7","elliptic_d10_pi9","elliptic_d10_pi10","elliptic_d11_pi12","elliptic_d12_pi13","elliptic_d12_pi14_ss_0","elliptic_d12_pi14_ss_inf";
  return(L);
}

proc surface_generic_catalogueType(string name)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_catalogueType(name);
RETURN:  one of rational, elliptic_ruled, Enriques, K3, bielliptic, abelian,
         elliptic, or unknown.
MATHEMATICS:
         This only records the surface type associated with a label; it is not
         an embedded Hilbert-polynomial classifier.
EXAMPLE: surface_generic_catalogueType(\"k3_d12_pi14\");
"
{
  if(name=="cubic_scroll") { return("rational_scroll"); }
  if(name=="veronese") { return("projected_veronese"); }
  if(name=="castelnuovo") { return("rational"); }
  if(name=="bordiga") { return("rational"); }
  if(name=="rational_d7_pi4") { return("rational"); }
  if(name=="rational_d8_pi5") { return("rational"); }
  if(name=="rational_d8_pi6") { return("rational"); }
  if(name=="rational_d9_pi6") { return("rational"); }
  if(name=="rational_d9_pi7") { return("rational"); }
  if(name=="rational_d10_pi8") { return("rational"); }
  if(name=="rational_d10_pi9_quart_1") { return("rational"); }
  if(name=="rational_d10_pi9_quart_2") { return("rational"); }
  if(name=="rational_d11_pi11_ss_0") { return("rational"); }
  if(name=="rational_d11_pi11_ss_1") { return("rational"); }
  if(name=="rational_d11_pi11_ss_inf") { return("rational"); }
  if(name=="quintic_elliptic_scroll") { return("elliptic_ruled"); }
  if(name=="enriques_d9_pi6") { return("Enriques"); }
  if(name=="enriques_d10_pi8") { return("Enriques"); }
  if(name=="enriques_d11_pi10") { return("Enriques"); }
  if(name=="enriques_d13_pi16") { return("Enriques"); }
  if(name=="enriques_d13_pi16_two") { return("Enriques"); }
  if(name=="k3_d7_pi5") { return("K3"); }
  if(name=="k3_d8_pi6") { return("K3"); }
  if(name=="k3_d9_pi8") { return("K3"); }
  if(name=="k3_d10_pi9_quart_1") { return("K3"); }
  if(name=="k3_d10_pi9_quart_2") { return("K3"); }
  if(name=="k3_d11_pi11_ss_0") { return("K3"); }
  if(name=="k3_d11_pi11_ss_1") { return("K3"); }
  if(name=="k3_d11_pi11_ss_2") { return("K3"); }
  if(name=="k3_d11_pi11_ss_3") { return("K3"); }
  if(name=="k3_d11_pi12") { return("K3"); }
  if(name=="k3_d12_pi14") { return("K3"); }
  if(name=="k3_d13_pi16") { return("K3"); }
  if(name=="k3_d14_pi19") { return("K3"); }
  if(name=="bielliptic_d10_pi6") { return("bielliptic"); }
  if(name=="bielliptic_d15_pi21") { return("bielliptic"); }
  if(name=="abelian_d10_pi6") { return("abelian"); }
  if(name=="abelian_d15_pi21_quintic_3") { return("abelian"); }
  if(name=="abelian_d15_pi21_quintic_1") { return("abelian"); }
  if(name=="elliptic_d7_pi6") { return("elliptic"); }
  if(name=="elliptic_d8_pi7") { return("elliptic"); }
  if(name=="elliptic_d9_pi7") { return("elliptic"); }
  if(name=="elliptic_d10_pi9") { return("elliptic"); }
  if(name=="elliptic_d10_pi10") { return("elliptic"); }
  if(name=="elliptic_d11_pi12") { return("elliptic"); }
  if(name=="elliptic_d12_pi13") { return("elliptic"); }
  if(name=="elliptic_d12_pi14_ss_0") { return("elliptic"); }
  if(name=="elliptic_d12_pi14_ss_inf") { return("elliptic"); }
  return("unknown");
}

proc surface_generic_catalogueSurface(string name, list #)
"USAGE:   surface_generic_catalogueSurface(name[,parameters]);
RETURN:  no return; use surface_generic_catalogueType and a direct constructor instead.
MATHEMATICS:
         This legacy generic dispatcher is disabled. Choose the direct
         constructor corresponding to surface_generic_catalogueType(name). For a fixed
         example, nonGeneralTypeSurfacesP4.lib provides nonGeneralTypeSurfaceP4.
EXAMPLE: surface_generic_catalogueType(\"abelian_d10_pi6\");
"
{
  string t=surface_generic_catalogueType(name);
  if(t=="unknown") { ERROR("surface_generic_catalogueSurface: unknown label"); }
  ERROR("surface_generic_catalogueSurface cannot safely dispatch ring-returning constructors; call surface_generic_catalogueType(name) and then a direct surface_generic_* constructor");
}

// Short aliases for the catalogue labels.  Each alias has its own doc string,
// so help output remains meaningful even when the function is not the preferred
// public entry point.

proc surface_generic_veronese(list #)
"USAGE: surface_generic_veronese([bound]); RETURN: generic projected Veronese rational surface. MATHEMATICS: image of P^2 by a general subsystem of conics. EXAMPLE: ring r=31991,(dummy),dp; list S=surface_generic_veronese();
"
{ return(surface_generic_projected_veronese(#)); }

proc surface_generic_castelnuovo(list #)
"USAGE: surface_generic_castelnuovo([bound]); RETURN: generic rational surface model. MATHEMATICS: returns a generic rational representative of the Castelnuovo-type rational class; exact P^4 Hilbert data require a specialized plane blow-up parameter space. EXAMPLE: ring r=31991,(dummy),dp; list S=surface_generic_castelnuovo();
"
{ return(surface_generic_rational_surface(#)); }

proc surface_generic_bordiga(list #)
"USAGE: surface_generic_bordiga([bound]); RETURN: generic rational surface model. MATHEMATICS: returns a generic rational representative of the Bordiga-type rational class; exact Bordiga embeddings are quartic plane blow-up models through ten points. EXAMPLE: ring r=31991,(dummy),dp; list S=surface_generic_bordiga();
"
{ return(surface_generic_rational_surface(#)); }

proc surface_generic_quintic_elliptic_scroll(list #)
"USAGE: surface_generic_quintic_elliptic_scroll([lambda]); RETURN: generic ruled surface over an elliptic curve. MATHEMATICS: E x P^1 has the same ruled-over-elliptic Kodaira type; the special quintic P^4 scroll embedding is not imposed. EXAMPLE: ring r=31991,(dummy),dp; list S=surface_generic_quintic_elliptic_scroll();
"
{ return(surface_generic_elliptic_ruled(#)); }

